  "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+5}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{4x+5}+2-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+4x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+4x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{\u2032}(x)=2x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{\u2033}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=1.$",
